WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.090
Film Courage: What are some common mistakes
that writers make in the first two stages

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:09.620
of writing a screenplay which compromises
the first act and then subsequent acts?

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:13.190
What are the first parts that end up compromising
the entire work?

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:16.630
Michael Hauge: I would actually go a little
further with your question.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:22.560
I would say, if you have problems in the first
two stages, it’s impossible for your script

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:23.560
to work.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:28.880
I have this sort of motto and that is “If
you’re having story problems, all roads

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.870
lead to the hero’s outer motivation.”

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:38.280
Because one of the biggest issues, one of
the most difficult things for writers to really

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:46.360
embrace is the idea that at the foundation
of any story is a visible goal that the hero

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.290
wants to cross at the end of the story.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:54.570
They’ll get so caught up in the inner journey,
in the depth of dimension of the character

00:00:54.570 --> 00:01:00.530
or in the themes they want to deliver or they’ll
just think the premise is good, so they’ll

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:07.650
get lost in a thicket of events and plotting
until finally they are just coming up with

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:13.090
new ideas and adding idea after idea until
it becomes so complicated and confusing that

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.000
there is no story there.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.940
If you think about most Hollywood movies in
particular are based on very simple story

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:21.940
ideas.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:28.220
Even if you take a story idea that is fairly
complex like INCEPTION.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.420
That seems pretty complicated.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.570
I have a little trouble grasping some of that
but at it’s very core, it’s very simple.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:39.740
A group of people want to penetrate a person’s
dreams down to a layer where they can change

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.619
behavior without him knowing it.

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:42.680
That’s it!

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:48.500
I was able to say that in what, five seconds
and one sentence?Everything is built on that

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.500
goal.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.890
And if you have that kind of clear through
line and if when you say what that goal is,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:01.930
everybody gets the goals of what achieving
that goal is, then you’ve laid a foundation

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:05.270
for what most scripts don’t honestly have.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:10.700
It’s odd because it sounds simple but it’s
not easy.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:16.690
I think for those of us who write or are storytellers,
we so want to get into character.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:18.950
We so want to get into deeper levels of meaning.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:25.110
We so want it to be original and different
and complex and special that we lose sight

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.890
of first I have to have this very simple throughline.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.330
So, that is the number one thing.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:34.530
One of the biggest problems in one and two
is that it doesn’t lead to a clear, visible

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:40.690
finish line that anyone can envision, whether
they are reading the script or watching the

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:43.780
movie, they know what they’re looking for.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:48.489
Second thing is not showing the everyday life
of the character before they get into the

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:50.569
story moving forward.

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:51.750
We have to have a set-up.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:56.710
It can be very brief or the character can
be on-the-move, so to speak, action wise.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:02.519
They can’t have just arrived into town and
we see them going to a meeting or going to

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:05.190
meet with a hit man or whatever it might be.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:10.019
Nonetheless, it has to convey that this is
who this character has been for some time

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:12.190
and it has to create empathy.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:13.570
Film Courage: Like Rambo.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:15.640
He arrives….Brian Dennehy’s character
sees him….

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.020
Michael Hauge: Yes, exactly.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:23.060
So we’ll find out what happened prior to
that but the first big event that is going

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.810
to happen to him doesn’t happen on page
one.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:31.430
We first have to get acquainted and identify
or empathize with this character.

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.920
And that empathy must occur immediately.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:42.019
We have to empathize before we see what the
flaws to the character are, before we start

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:47.879
recognizing what this inner conflict is or
what this identity is or what dark places

00:03:47.879 --> 00:03:49.489
they might go.

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:54.130
First we have to just connect with them emotionally.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:04:03.440
The third thing I would say is jumping from
the set-up to the outer motivation.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.910
I think because screenwriters are inundated
with the idea that you’ve got to grab the

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.970
reader right away and you’ve got to get
things going and Hollywood moves fast and

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:18.829
so on….all of that is true bit it doesn’t
mean you rush the story.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:22.360
What it means is you start building in conflict
as soon as you can.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:23.770
But you don’t rush the story.

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:28.949
It’s going to take all of act one to get
your hero to the point where they really begin

00:04:28.949 --> 00:04:31.400
pursuing that visible finish line.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:38.599
And if you start at page ten with them pursuing
the goal, your script is going to die around

00:04:38.599 --> 00:04:42.599
page 7, because you can’t sustain a single
goal that long.

00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:48.189
So you’ve got to take the whole stage one
and stage two just to build up, to create

00:04:48.189 --> 00:04:54.509
the opportunity in the new situation, to get
them oriented, where they have the goal and

00:04:54.509 --> 00:04:58.050
then they take that first step and that is
what has to happen at 25%.

